# ---------------- Loot.events (Task 9 F6: weighted loot tables) ----------------
# Weighted drop tables. `spawn item from "Loot" at me` picks a row and spawns that item;
# `roll "Rare" from "BossLoot"` does the same at the acting actor with a named rarity. The roll
# itself is ItemGenerator's (ItemData + AffixData), so a table drop and a hand-written
# `spawn Ring at me rarity "Rare" level 12` produce exactly the same item.
#
# HOW IT LOADS: read by name — Loot.events — when events load and on hot reload, before any
# actor's `me appears`, exactly like ItemData.events. Attaching it to a sheet is optional.
#
# FIELDS (per row, one variable each — the row name plus the field):
#   <Row>Table     which table the row belongs to ("Loot")
#   <Row>Base      the item base it drops — a name ItemData.events defines (Ring, Amulet, Sword)
#   <Row>Weight    the row's share of its table; a row at 0 (or with no weight) never drops
#   <Row>Rarity    optional: the rarity this row always drops ("Rare"). Absent, a drop uses the
#                  word's rarity, else Magic.
#
# THE ITEM LEVEL comes from the dropper's own `my Level` — an `enemy type` writes it — so an
# enemy sheet says `spawn item from "Loot" at me` and nothing else. With no level on the actor,
# the `ItemLevel` global is used, then 1. Weights are relative: 50/30/20 is a 50%/30%/20% split.

when game starts
  # ---- the ordinary drop table ---------------------------------------------------------------
  set TrinketTable to "Loot"
  set TrinketBase to "Ring"
  set TrinketWeight to 50
  set TrinketRarity to "Magic"

  set CharmTable to "Loot"
  set CharmBase to "Amulet"
  set CharmWeight to 30
  set CharmRarity to "Magic"

  set BladeTable to "Loot"
  set BladeBase to "Sword"
  set BladeWeight to 20
  set BladeRarity to "Magic"

  # ---- a boss table: one row, always a rare ring ---------------------------------------------
  set BossRingTable to "BossLoot"
  set BossRingBase to "Ring"
  set BossRingWeight to 1
  set BossRingRarity to "Rare"
